{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Because they did not meet you with bread and water</b>. We find (above 2:28–29): 'Sell me food for money, that I may eat, and give me water for money, that I may drink, only as the children of Esav who dwell in Se'ir did for me, and the Moavim who dwell in Ar.' Many say that they [the Moavim] did not meet them [with hospitality] but Israel purchased from them, and this is nothing, for it suffices for a camp that they sell to them when they wish to purchase. Moreover, Israel did not enter the territory of Moav at all, and the Moavim brought out bread and water to them for money outside their border, and Scripture recounts that the Moavim did as the children of Esav did — so why would He exclude the Moavi forever for this but not abominate the Edomi? And Rabbi Avraham [Ibn Ezra] said (ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 2:29) that the sense of 'as the children of Esav and the Moavim did for me' is [in reference to] 'I will pass through your land by the highway' (ibid. v. 27), but they did not sell them bread and water, for they [Israel] passed through the mountain of Se'ir and through Ar, but the king of Edom did not let them pass through the territory of his state where he dwells, as it says (Numbers 20:18), 'You shall not pass through me.' This too is nothing, for they said to the king of Edom, 'I will pass through your land,' and 'Edom refused to give Israel passage through his border' (ibid. v. 21) — behold, they did not enter the territory of Edom at all. And likewise it is written (ibid. 21:4), 'And they journeyed from Mount Hor by the way of the Red Sea, to go around the land of Edom,' for they were forced to turn back toward the Red Sea from Mount Hor, which is on the border of the land of Edom, and they did not enter the land of Edom itself at all. And Yiftach said in his explanation (Judges 11:16–18): 'But when Israel came up from Egypt, and Israel walked through the wilderness unto the Red Sea, and came to Kadesh, and Israel sent messengers unto the king of Edom, saying, Let me pass through your land, and the king of Edom hearkened not, and also to the king of Moav he sent and he would not consent, and Israel dwelt in Kadesh, and walked through the wilderness, and went around the land of Edom and the land of Moav,' and so on, 'and they pitched on the other side of Arnon,' and did not enter the territory of Moav. Behold, this is explicit that they did not enter the land of Edom at all, nor the land of Moav, and had they entered there, [the inhabitants] would have sold them bread and water, for it is not the custom to grant permission to an army to pass through one's land without selling them bread and water. \\n\\nAnd what appears correct to me is that Scripture excluded these two brothers, who were recipients of kindness from Avraham, who saved their father and their mother from the sword and from captivity (Genesis 14:16), and in whose merit HaShem sent them forth from the midst of the overthrow (ibid. 19:29), and they were obligated to do good with Israel, yet they did evil with them — the one [Moav] hired against him Bila'am son of Be'or, and these are the Moavim, and the one [Amon] did not meet him with bread and water when they drew near opposite him, as it is written (above 2:18–19), 'You are to pass over the border of Moav, Ar, today, and you will draw near opposite the children of Amon,' and behold Scripture warned them (ibid. v. 19), 'Do not besiege them, and do not provoke them,' and they [Amon] did not meet them at all. For Scripture says, 'As the children of Esav did for me, [and as] the Moavim and the Amonim,' but it did not mention Amon among those who failed to meet them. And behold Amon offended in this more than all of them, for the children of Esav and the Moavim, when they knew that Israel had been warned not to provoke them, brought out bread and water outside their borders, but Amon refused to do so. And this is the sense of <b>because they did not meet you</b> — that they did not go out to meet them with bread and water as the others did. Therefore Scripture set <b>Amoni</b> first and first mentioned his transgression — 'because they did not meet you' — and afterward mentioned <b>Moavi</b> and his sin. \\n\\nAnd our Sages interpreted (Yevamot 76a): 'Amoni and not Amonit, Moavi and not Moavit,' because it is the way of a man to go out [to meet], but it is not the way of a woman to go out and bring out food outside the border of the land. And so they mentioned in the Yerushalmi (ibid. ch. 8 hal. 3) and in Megillat Ruth Rabbati (4:8): because it is the way of a man to hire, but it is not the way of a woman to hire."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The word הבל, used by Ramban to dismiss others' interpretations, is rendered literally as 'nothing,' preserving his forceful rejection without softening."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:54:10.127469014Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}